dressable via prompt engineering, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, model editing, and reinforcement learning.\nL17: We introduce a unified framework encompassing prompt-level steering, activation interventions, and weight-space edits. We analyze robustness and safety implications, including adversarial attacks and alignment mitigations. Theoretically, we show minimal weight updates can achieve targeted behavior changes with limited side-effects. Empirically, we demonstrate >90% success in sentiment control and factual edits while preserving base performance, though generalization-specificity trade-offs exist.\nL18: We discuss ethical dual-use risks and the need for rigorous evaluation. This work lays groundwork for designing controllable and robust language models.\nL19: Comments:  | 13 pages\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 68T50, 68T05\nL22: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.2.7; I.2.6; I.2.11\nL23: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2509.04549 [cs.CL]\nL24:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2509.04549v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL25:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.04549†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Taylan Alpay [cite12†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Thu, 4 Sep 2025 17:32:56 UTC (15 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Manipulating Transformer-Based Models: Controllability, Steerability, and Robust Interventions, by Faruk Alpay and 1 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite7†View PDF L38:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite13†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite14†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L47: \nL48: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2025-09 L49: \nL50: Change to brows